<p>South African President Cyril Ramaphosa said he would not resign after the Constitutional Court revived parliamentary impeachment proceedings tied to the 2020 theft of foreign currency hidden in furniture at his Phala Phala farm, according to accounts of the case. In a televised address on May 11, Ramaphosa said the ruling did not require him to step down and said he would defend himself against allegations that he mishandled the matter. The case returned to Parliament after the Constitutional Court set aside the National Assembly’s 2022 decision to block referral of an independent panel report to an impeachment committee, and Parliament moved to establish a committee to reinvestigate allegations of serious misconduct. Ramaphosa has consistently denied stealing public money, committing a crime or violating his oath of office, and said he would seek judicial review of the Section 89 panel report, which he called flawed.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/ljbOJLiaPxGWMeyjmJqmR8RiPA7U8_7kMQOuth5XbULwpxnuMbVsudXYVN3H-W4QsFEp-CLyMLxAx2jcU9DV3sBM6SyI_Bb77-V44y4IGVYU6I-hWOj-fMYAwfulU1bPvB8PYBAMC6U53VV8jh9iL_ob8xbK' alt='Cyril Ramaphosa addresses South Africans after the Constitutional Court ruling on the Phala Phala impeachment process.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Court ruling: The Constitutional Court ruled that parts of the National Assembly rules on removing a president were inconsistent with the Constitution and invalidated Rule 129I.</li><li>Scope of judgment: Ramaphosa said the Constitutional Court made no finding of any kind regarding his alleged conduct.</li><li>Cash amount: The panel report cited an alleged theft of about $580,000, approximately R9.6 million, from the Phala Phala game farm in Limpopo in February 2020.</li><li>ANC response: ANC officials prepared to defend Ramaphosa after his address, with party figures signaling they were ready to take up the fight.</li></ul><blockquote>I, therefore, respectfully want to make it clear that I will not resign. To do so would be to preempt a process defined by the Constitution. To do so would be to give credence to a panel report that unfortunately has grave flaws. - Cyril Ramaphosa</blockquote><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Cyril Ramaphosa: Ramaphosa said he remained committed to serving South Africans and completing the mandate entrusted to him as president. (<a href='https://www.sanews.gov.za/south-africa/i-remain-here-i-will-not-resign-president-ramaphosa'>SAnews.gov.za</a>)</li><li>Economic Freedom Fighters: EFF spokesperson Sinawo Thambo said Ramaphosa should resign if he had &quot;any conscience, political integrity, or respect for the people of South Africa&quot;. (<a href='https://www.thesouthafrican.com/news/eff-reacts-to-ramaphosas-decision-to-remain-in-office/'>The South African</a>)</li><li>Parliament Speaker Thoko Didiza: Didiza said Parliament would republish the 2022 Section 89 report and table it before the National Assembly as it carried out the court order. (<a href='https://www.africaninsider.com/politics/impeachment-inquiry-into-ramaphosa-begins-in-parliament/'>African Insider</a>)</li><li>ANC chair Gwede Mantashe: Mantashe said Ramaphosa would not take shortcuts in dealing with Phala Phala, according to IOL&#x27;s account of his remarks. (<a href='https://www.iol.co.za/news/politics/he-will-not-take-shortcuts-mantashe-says-ramaphosa-will-not-resign-over-phala-phala-22b09a68-8faf-49b7-a539-7994b5626e08'>IOL</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.scmp.com/news/world/africa/article/3353196/south-africas-ramaphosa-faces-down-calls-resign-over-cash-sofa-scandal'>South Africa’s Ramaphosa defies calls to resign over ‘cash-in-sofa’ scandal</a> - scmp.com</li><li><a href='https://abcnews.com/International/wireStory/south-africa-establish-impeachment-committee-after-presidents-cash-132849206'>Ramaphosa says he will not resign as South Africa opens an impeachment committee over cash scandal</a> - abcnews.com</li><li><a href='https: